<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin-bottom: 10px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> What's that Sound?</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> The Cat Says 'Meow'.</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: reading_1: Recognize basic familiar words supported with illustrations or visual aids.
reading_2: Match spoken words to their written form as they are heard and supported with pictures.
phonemic_awareness: Recognize familiar letters.</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td colspan="2" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can recognize basic familiar words related to domestic animals, supported by illustrations or visual aids (e.g., saying 'rooster' when hearing the sound of a rooster and seeing the word 'rooster' with a picture).
Can match spoken words to their written form when supported by pictures (e.g., matching flashcards with pictures of domestic animals, such as a 'rooster,' to the corresponding written word).
Can identify letters at the beginning of familiar animal names (e.g., C for 'cat').</p></td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 15px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f0f8ff;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts with a "What Animal Am I?" game. The teacher plays animal sounds (e.g., 'meow', 'woof', 'quack') from Lesson 1 and shows corresponding animal picture flashcards. Students listen, identify the sound, and point to the correct animal picture. The teacher asks, "What sound is this?" and students respond with the animal name and sound, e.g., "Cat! Meow!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to connect a picture to a word. The teacher holds up a flashcard with a 'cat' picture and then a separate flashcard with the word 'cat'. The teacher points to the picture, says "cat," then points to the word 'cat' and says "cat." The teacher repeats for 'dog' and 'rooster', clearly articulating the words. The teacher also models the initial letter sound for 'cat' (/k/) and 'rooster' (/r/). "C for 'cat', R for 'rooster'."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the spoken words and pictures.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing 'dog' picture and saying "cat"): "Is this a cat?" (Expected: "No!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing 'cat' picture and the word 'dog'): "Does this word say 'cat'?" (Expected: "No!")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "What's that Sound?".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An illustrated "Animal Sound Story" in a big book format or large individual flashcards. Each page features a large picture of a domestic animal (cat, dog, rooster, cow, duck, sheep, horse, pig, bird) and a simple, repetitive sentence, e.g., "This is a cat. The cat says meow." or "Look! A dog. The dog says woof." Genre: Short illustrated narrative/rhyme. Register: Informal, simple. Format: Big book or large flashcards with text + picture.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Recognize basic familiar words related to domestic animals, supported by illustrations or visual aids. Reading: Match spoken words to their written form as they are heard and supported by pictures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activities:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher introduces the "Animal Sound Story" big book. "Look! It's an animal book! What animals do you see?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the story aloud, pointing to each animal picture and then to the corresponding animal word in the sentence. For example, pointing to the 'cat' picture, then the word 'cat' as they read, "This is a <b>cat</b>. The cat says <b>meow</b>."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher emphasizes the target vocabulary (cat, dog, rooster, cow, duck, sheep, horse, pig, bird) and the verbs 'says', encouraging students to listen for the sound and see the word.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> After reading a page, "What animal is this?" (Students point to the picture). "What sound does it make?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching:</b> The teacher displays flashcards with only animal words (e.g., 'cat', 'dog'). As the story is re-read, when the teacher says "cat", students try to point to the word 'cat' flashcard.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading basic familiar animal words (cat, dog, rooster, cow, duck, sheep, horse, pig, bird). Grammar: Present simple ('The cat says meow.'), Exclamations ('That's a rooster!'). Vocabulary: nouns (cat, dog, rooster, bird, cow, duck, sheep, horse, fish, pig), verbs (say), animal sounds (bark, meow, quack, woof). Phonemic awareness: Recognizing letters 'C', 'D', 'R' at the beginning of familiar animal names.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Word-to-Picture Match (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher distributes worksheets with three columns: pictures of domestic animals, the written names of the animals (scrambled order), and the initial letter of the animal (scrambled order). Students draw lines to match each animal picture (e.g., cat) to its correct written word ('cat') and then to its initial letter ('C'). The teacher guides them by pointing to a picture and saying the word, then asking students to find the word. "Find the cat!" "Now find the 'C'!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. "Hear and Read" Flashcards (Semi-controlled Practice):</b> The teacher prepares large flashcards with only the written animal names (e.g., 'CAT', 'DOG', 'ROOSTER'). The teacher says an animal sound (e.g., "Woof! Woof!"), and students listen, then pick up and hold high the flashcard with the matching animal word ('DOG'). The teacher then asks a student to *read* the word aloud. This activity reinforces matching spoken input to written words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. "Letter Spotter" Game (Phonemic Awareness / Pair Check):</b> Students work in pairs with a set of small animal word cards (e.g., 'CAT', 'DOG', 'PIG', 'ROOSTER', 'DUCK'). One student picks a card and *reads* the word aloud if they can, or identifies the picture. The other student's job is to *point to the first letter* of the word and say its sound, e.g., "C! /k/ for cat!" "D! /d/ for dog!" The teacher circulates, checking for accurate word recognition and letter identification.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-17 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "What's that Sound?".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Animal Sound Reading Bingo". Students receive a Bingo card with a grid of familiar animal pictures (e.g., cat, dog, rooster, cow). The teacher has a bag of small word cards for these animals. The teacher pulls out a word card (e.g., 'CAT'), *shows it to the class*, and says the word clearly. Students must *read the word* on the teacher's card, find the matching animal picture on their Bingo card, and cover it with a marker. The first student to get three in a row (horizontally, vertically, or diagonally) shouts "Bingo!" and reads the three animal words they covered.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> This activity directly connects to "What's that Sound?" by requiring students to recognize the written word of an animal and match it to its picture, then recall its sound. The theme "The Cat Says 'Meow'" is embedded through the animal vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in recognizing familiar written animal words with visual aids, collaborative interaction (listening and checking with peers), and communicative task completion (getting Bingo).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on individual student's ability to recognize and match words to pictures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> "Read and Point." The teacher holds up a flashcard with an animal word (e.g., 'CAT'). Students are asked individually or in small groups to *read* the word and point to a large picture of the corresponding animal displayed on the board. The teacher can also say an animal sound (e.g., "Oink! Oink!"), and students *point to the written word* 'PIG'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognizes familiar words related to domestic animals (e.g., 'cat', 'dog', 'rooster') supported by illustrations.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Matches spoken animal sound/name to its written form when supported by pictures.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identifies familiar letters at the beginning of animal names (e.g., 'C' for 'cat', 'D' for 'dog').</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are given a small paper with three animal words (e.g., 'cat', 'dog', 'rooster') and a smiley face next to each. They circle the smiley face if they feel they can read that word. "Can you read 'cat'? Circle the happy face!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What animal words did you learn to read today?" (Students can point to a word on a chart or their exit ticket).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which letter did you see a lot today?" (Expected: C, D, R, P, Q).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> The reading activities were effective for Kinder, with multi-sensory support. The big book story engaged students, and the matching games provided good practice. Some students struggled with differentiating between 'P' and 'D' for 'pig' and 'dog'. More visual differentiation and explicit letter sound practice will be needed for these. The Bingo game was a hit and provided good fluency practice for word recognition. The next lesson will transition into speaking, using these newly recognized words in simple sentences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Next Lesson:</b> "Great job reading about the animals today! Next time, we will talk more about these animals and their sounds with our friends, and make our own animal sound conversations!" (Focus on Speaking & Interaction for Lesson 3).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>